        <p>LLM simulations of therapy patients for trainees. Founded with NHS CBT practitioners.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Machine Learning</h3>
        <p>BSc + MSc in machine learning at Aalto University, Finland.</p>
        <div class="meta"><span>Latent ethical landscape of LLMs</span><span>Gaussian Modelling, University of Helsinki</span><span>talk: transformers</span></div>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>ACL Workshop 2025</h3>
        <p>adding soon</p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section id="about" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
      <h2 id="about-heading">about</h2>
      <p>I care about AI being good for the people it touches. Two questions hold most of my attention:</p>
      <p class="question"><span>1</span> What's in the latent space, and can we learn to read it and reach in?</p>
      <p class="question"><span>2</span> What happens where a human and an AI meet, and how do we design that interface?</p>
      <p class="coda">Both questions point to something far more complex, subtle, and fertile than first appears, rewarding a kind of close attention I'm drawn towards.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="ideas" aria-labelledby="ideas-heading">
      <h2 id="ideas-heading">steal these ideas</h2>
      <p>Ideas I'd love to see exist but won't get to. Take any of them, no credit needed.</p>
      <ul class="plain-list">
        <li>A reader that shows the latent space of what you're reading</li>
        <li>Therapy-sim for hard conversations you're about to have</li>
        <li>An interface to "reach into" a model's reasoning and nudge it</li>
        <li>A personal site that's a place, not a poster</li>
        <li>Margin notes that argue back</li>
        <li>A bookshelf that remembers why you loved each book</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

